WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.650
Every AI agent tutorial you have watched is lying to you. They show you one agent, doing one task, in a straight line. Prompt in, answer out. Looks great in a demo.

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:23.219
Then you try to build something real — five agents, one goal — and it falls apart. Agents talk over each other. State gets lost. One agent loops forever and burns your API budget while you are asleep.

00:00:23.225 --> 00:00:30.445
There is a reason for that. And there is a fix. It is called graph engineering — and once you see it, you cannot unsee it.

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:45.330
Here is the mental shift. A single AI agent is not one call to a model. It is a loop. Think. Act. Look at the result. Think again. You cannot draw a loop with an arrow that only points forward. A straight line cannot come back on itself.

00:00:45.325 --> 00:00:55.505
So the moment you need looping — or branching, or five agents working at once — a straight chain breaks. You need a graph. Nodes. Edges. And something flowing between them.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:09.680
Every graph-based agent system runs on four things. One — state. A shared object every agent reads and writes to. Not 'the conversation.' Real structured data: the task, the plan, the results so far, and the errors.

00:01:09.674 --> 00:01:26.155
Two — nodes. Each one does one job: an LLM call, a tool call, or a human checkpoint. Nodes can contain entire agents inside them — nesting like Russian dolls. Three — edges. Conditional edges: a function that looks at the state and asks, 'what happens next?'

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:40.620
This — right here — is the actual engineering. Not the prompts. The routing. And four — checkpoints. Save state after every step. When an agent runs for ten minutes and step forty-one crashes, you do not want to lose steps one through forty.

00:01:40.625 --> 00:01:56.504
Okay. Here is how you actually go from one agent to an army. Pattern one: the Supervisor. Picture a general and five soldiers. The soldiers never talk to each other — they report back to the general, who decides who moves next. One brain, many hands.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:14.150
Pattern two: Fan-out, fan-in. This is the real army move. Say you need to research five competitors. You do not send one agent to do it five times in a row — you spawn five agents at once, each takes one competitor, and they all report back to a single reducer that merges the results.

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:29.030
But here is the trap almost everyone falls into: if two agents write to the same piece of state at the same time, and you have not told your system how to merge them — one silently erases the other's work. Nobody warns you about this. I am warning you.

00:02:29.025 --> 00:02:42.844
Pattern three: Hierarchical. Once you are past five or six agents, one supervisor gets overwhelmed. So supervisors get their own sub-supervisors. Teams of teams. That is how you actually scale this past a demo.

00:02:42.849 --> 00:02:55.349
Three things will bite you the first time you build this for real. Loops that never end — because you asked an AI 'are we done yet?' and it cannot decide. Fix: always cap it with a hard iteration limit.

00:02:55.349 --> 00:03:09.500
Cost that quietly explodes — because every hop through your graph is a full model call. And debugging — in a graph, you must log every state change at every step, or you will be flying blind. These are not edge cases. These are Tuesday.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:23.879
So here is the whole idea in one image: Stop thinking about your agents as a line. Start thinking about them as a map — with a shared memory, decision points at every fork, and a plan for what happens when five paths need to become one.

00:03:23.875 --> 00:03:37.375
The agents you have been building? They were never the hard part. The coordination between them — that is the actual skill. That is the part nobody teaches. And now you know it. If this made something click for you — go build your army.

